With these fixed parameters, suppose $k>2L$ and $$\label{sur:sensitivity}
 \operatorname{opt}_{k-2L}\le(1+\xi)\operatorname{opt}_k.$$ Put $h=k-L$. In polynomial time one can compute a real facility set $S$ of size $k$ with $$\label{sur:conclusion}
 \operatorname{cost}(S)
 \le\left(2-\frac{c}{100}\right)\operatorname{opt}_h
 \le\left(2-\frac{c}{100}\right)(1+\xi)
       \operatorname{opt}_k.$$ The algorithm does not need to test Equation <a href="#sur:sensitivity" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="sur:sensitivity">[sur:sensitivity]</a>.

outputs.exact_statement:
There is an absolute constant $\sigma>0$ with the following property. For every fixed $a>0$, a randomized polynomial-time algorithm for metric $k$-median always returns a set $S\subseteq F$ with $|S|\le k$ and satisfies $$\Pr\!\left\{\operatorname{cost}(S)
       \le(2-\sigma)\operatorname{opt}_k\right\}
 \ge 1-(|D|+|F|+2)^{-a}.$$ The same algorithm can be chosen to satisfy $\mathbb E\operatorname{cost}(S)\le(2-\sigma)\operatorname{opt}_k$. The running time is polynomial in the bit length of the finite rational metric input. Its degree may depend on the fixed constants.

The blind sampling procedure, run for at most $2m$ steps and retaining all prefixes, has probability at least $\exp(-O_\mu(m))$ of producing a prefix with the following properties.*




*There is an earlier prefix $H^0$, with covered set $A^1$ and remaining bad set $B^0$, at which the deleted set is the final set $Q$. Put $$R^*=S_0\setminus Q,\qquad
 J^0=\bigcup_{s\in R^*}J_s(H^0),\qquad
 T_i=\sum_{p\in N_i^1}d(p,H^0)\quad(i\in B^0).$$ Then $$\label{ref:first-stop}
 \sum_{i\in B^0}T_i\le\kappa P,
 \qquad \sum_{p\in J^0}d(p,H^0)\le\kappa P.$$ For each nonempty cluster $i\in B^0$, define $f_i=|C_i\cap J^0|/n_i$. All balls added after $H^0$ correspond to a set $A^2\subseteq\{i\in B^0:n_i>0,\ f_i<1/3\}$, and their leaders lie in $N_i^2$; the leaders for $A^1$ lie in $N_i^1$. At the final prefix, set $A=A^1\cup A^2$, $B=B^0\setminus A^2$, and $J=\bigcup_{s\in R^*}J_s(H)$. We have $$\label{ref:second-stop}
 J\subseteq J^0,\qquad
 \sum_{p\in J}d(p,H)\le\kappa P,\qquad
 \sum_{\substack{i\in B:n_i>0\\ f_i<1/3}}
       \sum_{p\in N_i^2}d(p,H)\le\kappa P.$$ Every ball satisfies Equation <a href="#ref:radius" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="ref:radius">[ref:radius]</a>, and the collection satisfies $$\label{ref:radius-sum}
 \sum_{i\in A}n_i r^0(w_i)\le\frac{11}{2}P.$$ The earlier prefix and the conditions defining it need not be recognized by the algorithm.

For the endpoint above, the cost bound in Equation <a href="#ref:completion-bound" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="ref:completion-bound">[ref:completion-bound]</a> is at most $$\label{ref:explicit-bound}
 \left(1+2\theta a_1+\mu+11\kappa+\frac\kappa q\right)P
 \le (1+2/e+6\mu)P.$$

